"""lkagent — an agent's own phone line on a LiveKit server: GPT Live is the voice and the
agent's own harness session in its folder is the backend it hands each turn to."""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path

HELP = """lkagent — an agent's own phone line on a LiveKit server (Cloud or self-hosted).

This is the menu; `lkagent help <verb>` is one verb's whole contract.

GPT Live, under client delegation, is the voice: it holds the conversation and hands each
turn it cannot answer to the line's backend, the agent's own harness session in its folder,
one session for the whole call. The call's lifecycle - how it opens, how long it may run,
a silent line, hanging up, reporting a finished call to a webhook - is callva-livekit's.
The worker takes one call at a time, and records each call when it ends.

MODEL
  A connection is one LiveKit server: its URL, API key and API secret. The worker
  registers on it under the line's agent name, and answers only what is dispatched to
  that name: an inbound call through a SIP dispatch rule naming it, an outbound call
  placed by `lkagent call`. Buying a number and the SIP trunks and dispatch rules are set
  up in LiveKit and the carrier (`lkagent guide twilio-number`); this capability reads
  them and never changes them.

  The line's numbers are its settings (`numbers`). A call is taken only on one of them -
  an inbound call on the number it called, an outbound one on the number it is placed
  from - and the backend runs under that number's instructions and rights: the
  capabilities it may run and whether it is read-only. A call on any other number is
  hung up at once and recorded as failed.

  Each finished call is a row of lkagent_calls in the project's store: who, which way,
  when, how it ended, the whole transcript and the call.ended body the webhook gets.
  A project with no store records nothing and says so once on the service log.

COMMANDS
  Readiness
    lkagent doctor                 is the line ready: connection, key, backend, routing
    lkagent service doctor         what the worker would start with, without starting it

  Calls
    lkagent call <number>          place an outgoing call from one of the line's numbers

  The worker
    lkagent service run            run the worker in the foreground until stopped

THE WRITE GATE
  `call` reaches a person outside the system, so it needs the connection's write grant
  (WRITE_DEFAULT is false). Ask the user; never lift a gate yourself.

EXIT CODES
  0 success · 2 credentials (a LiveKit key, secret or the OpenAI key missing or refused)
  · 3 not found · 4 policy (the project gate, or `call` on a connection without
  allow_write) · 5 network (LiveKit did not answer, or refused the dispatch) · 6 input
  (bad arguments, an ambiguous or unconfigured number, settings it cannot read)
"""

_HELP_SETTINGS = """
  The line's settings are read when the worker starts; restart it after a change.
  Rights have one home, the number: `numbers` is a list of objects
    {"number": "+<country><number>", "instructions": "<text>",
     "allowed_capabilities": ["<capability>", ...] or ["*"], "read_only": true|false}
  A call on a number is bound by that entry: its instructions are added to the backend's
  system prompt after backend_prompt, allowed_capabilities is what the backend session
  may run (none when empty, every one with "*"), and read_only puts it under the
  read-only switch. A number is matched on its digits.

  The OpenAI key GPT Live runs on is the machine's OPENAI_API_KEY (`lkagent help setup`),
  resolved from the agent's vault first; it is no setting.

  Examples:
    lkagent settings set numbers '[{"number": "+15550100", "instructions": "Answer for the front desk.", "allowed_capabilities": ["tasks"], "read_only": false}]'
    lkagent settings set outbound_trunk ST_<id>
    lkagent settings set recording true
"""

HELP_VERBS = {
    "doctor": """\
  lkagent doctor [--connection <id>] [--offline]
      Is the line ready: the connection's values, the OpenAI key, the backend harness,
      the line's numbers, and (unless --offline) LiveKit accepting the key and secret
      (rooms listed) and the SIP dispatch rules that route to the line's agent name,
      with the phone numbers they route. Read-only; never prints a secret.

  Output: {ok, checks: {connection, openai_key, backend, line, livekit?, sip?},
  warnings: [...]}. `sip` is {agent_name, rules: [{id, name, kind, trunk_ids,
  numbers}], numbers, rules_read, inbound_trunks_read}; it reads LiveKit's SIP service
  (ListSIPDispatchRule, ListSIPInboundTrunk). A rule routes to the line when its room
  configuration dispatches the line's agent name; its numbers are its own, else those of
  the inbound trunks it applies to. No rule routing to the line, or a server with no SIP
  service, is a warning, not a failure. LiveKit's API does not give the project's SIP
  URI; `lkagent guide twilio-number` says where it is.

  Exit 0 ready / 2 a value missing or refused / 5 is not used: an unreachable server is
  `livekit.ok: false`, exit 2.

  Examples:
    lkagent doctor
    lkagent doctor --offline
""",
    "call": """\
  lkagent call <number> [--from <line number>] [--task <text>] [--connection <id>]
      Place an outgoing call: dispatch the worker into a new room with the line's call
      configuration, the task as the {{task}} variable, and dial <number> through the
      `outbound_trunk` setting with --from as the caller id. --from is one of the line's
      `numbers`; without it the only configured number is used. The call runs under that
      number's instructions and rights. Needs the worker running (`lkagent service run`)
      and the connection's write grant.

  <number> and --from are given as +<country code><number>.
  Output: {room, dispatch_id, agent_name, number, from}.
  Exit 0 dispatched / 2 credentials missing / 4 no write grant / 5 LiveKit refused the
  dispatch or the dial / 6 a bad number, no number configured, --from not one of the
  line's numbers or several configured and none named (ambiguous_number), no
  outbound_trunk, or the worker never prepared the line.

  Example:
    lkagent call +15550123 --from +15550100 --task "Confirm tomorrow's delivery window."
""",
    "service run": """\
  lkagent service run [--dev]
      Run the worker in the foreground in this agent's folder until stopped: register
      on the connection's LiveKit server under the line's agent name and answer every
      call dispatched to it, one at a time. It first brings lkagent_calls up to date in
      the project's store; without a store it runs and records no call, saying so once.
      --dev runs LiveKit's development mode (reload on change, verbose logs).

  Needs the project to enable lkagent explicitly, the connection, OPENAI_API_KEY, and
  a `claude` or `codex` the backend runs on. Settings are read as it starts.
  Exit 0 stopped / 2 credentials missing / 4 not enabled in this project / 6 input.
""",
    "service doctor": """\
  lkagent service doctor [--connection <id>]
      What `service run` would start with, without starting it: the agent name, the
      server URL, the agent's folder and state directory, the settings, the
      connection, whether outgoing calls have a trunk, the line's numbers, and whether
      calls are recorded (the store resolves and the project has an agent id). Local;
      no secret printed.

  Output: {ok, agent_name, url, agent_root, state_dir, settings, connection, outbound,
  numbers, calls_recorded: {recorded, reason}}.
  Exit 0 / 2 credentials missing / 6 input.
""",
    "settings": _HELP_SETTINGS,
}

